+Feature: DOM identity
+  As a browser user
+  I want DOM elements to stay attached across interactions
+  So that assistive technology, focus, and event chains in real browsers never break
+
+  Rule: The app MUST NOT replace DOM nodes when mutating todo state.
+        Instead, it MUST mutate the existing node in place — toggle class names,
+        checked properties, and text content — so the browser element reference
+        remains valid across the interaction.
+
+  Background:
+    Given the app is loaded
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: The source code does not use full-list innerHTML replacement
+    When I inspect the source of "js/app.js"
+    Then the source MUST NOT contain a full-list re-render pattern that destroys all child elements
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Single-todo completed toggle preserves the <li> element identity
+    Given the todo list has 1 item with title "Buy milk"
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I click the toggle checkbox on the todo
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And the todo has class "completed"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Single-todo uncompleted toggle preserves the <li> element identity
+    Given the todo list has 1 completed item with title "Buy milk"
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I click the toggle checkbox on the todo
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And the todo does not have class "completed"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Toggle-all completed preserves <li> element identities
+    Given the todo list has items "Buy milk", "Buy eggs", "Buy bread"
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I click the "toggle-all" checkbox
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And all todos have class "completed"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Toggle-all clear preserves <li> element identities
+    Given the todo list has 3 completed items
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I click the "toggle-all" checkbox
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And no todo has class "completed"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Entering edit mode preserves the <li> element identity
+    Given the todo list has 1 item with title "Buy milk"
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I double-click the todo label
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And the todo has class "editing"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Saving an edit preserves the <li> element identity
+    Given the todo list has 1 item with title "Buy milk"
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I double-click the todo label
+    And I change the edit input to "Buy organic milk"
+    And I press Enter in the edit input
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And the todo list shows 1 item with title "Buy organic milk"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Cancelling an edit preserves the <li> element identity
+    Given the todo list has 1 item with title "Buy milk"
+    When I capture the reference of the first todo element
+    And I double-click the todo label
+    And I change the edit input to "Buy organic milk"
+    And I press Escape in the edit input
+    Then the first todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And the todo list shows 1 item with title "Buy milk"
+
+  @contract
+  Scenario: Destroying a todo preserves surrounding <li> element identities
+    Given the todo list has items "Buy milk", "Buy eggs"
+    When I capture the reference of the second todo element
+    And I click the destroy button on the todo titled "Buy milk"
+    Then the second todo element is still the same DOM node
+    And the todo list shows 1 item with title "Buy eggs"
